{"course_material": "[STAT101-W3-N3] Degrees of freedom and Bessel's correction
The n-1 divisor is known as Bessel's correction, and n-1 is the degrees of freedom of the sample variance. Intuition: once x-bar is fixed, only n-1 of the n deviations can vary freely — the last one is determined because the deviations must sum to zero. So the sample supplies n-1, not n, independent pieces of information about spread.

[ST...[truncated 762 chars]", "question": "On problem set 2 I lost points for computing the variance with n in the denominator. Why do we divide by n-1 instead of n when we compute the sample variance, and what does 'degrees of freedom' have to do with it?"}

